an>Secret deals, tight control</span></h2><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px=
 georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">=
So, Yu Young, tell me about NewJeans. Who are they, and what did they do th=
at got so much attention=3F</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px geo=
rgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>NewJeans is one of the biggest=
 K-pop bands of the last few years. They released their debut song in 2022,=
 and it immediately shot up the charts. Industry insiders loved them becaus=
e they had this really clean image. They were a bunch of fresh-faced teenag=
ers singing about coming of age and having crushes.</span></p><p style=3D"f=
ont:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But Ne=
wJeans hasn=E2=80=99t performed in a year, and it hasn=E2=80=99t made any n=
ew music. Today, part of what the singers are famous for is the fact that t=
hey tried to quit their label =E2=80=94 and weren=E2=80=99t allowed to. The=
 label took them to court, and the court ordered NewJeans members to serve =
out their contracts.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,se=
rif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">Wow. So, Sa=
rah, why was this such a big deal=3F</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px=
/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>By trying to quit =E2=
=80=94 and being barred from doing so =E2=80=94 NewJeans trained a spotligh=
t on what many see as the controlling and exploitative contracts that are p=
ervasive in K-pop. That=E2=80=99s kind of what prompted our investigation. =
We wanted to see what was in these secret contracts. Eventually we got hold=
 of them, and what we found really surprised us.</span></p><p style=3D"font=
:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"=
font-weight:700">What was in them, Yu Young=3F</span></p><p style=3D"font:n=
ormal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Among other=
 terms, the contracts bar group members from criticizing the label, even if=
 what they say is true. They=E2=80=99re not allowed to have =E2=80=9Cinappr=
opriate romantic relationships=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Cinsincere attitudes.=E2=
=80=9D Another clause states that once the contracts expire, the label will=
 sharply reduce the album and streaming revenues it pays to the members. No=
t only that, the label retains the right to use their voices, faces and ide=
ntities, which could be used to sell new content generated by A.I.</span></=
p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15p=
x"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">Creepy!</span></p><p style=3D"font:norma=
l 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>And remember, t=
he members of NewJeans are very young. They=E2=80=99re mostly in their earl=
y 20s now, but they all signed these contracts when they were minors.</span=
></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 =
15px"><span>Another feature of the contracts: high exit fees. Four of the f=
ive NewJeans members were forced to serve out their contracts, but one had =
hers terminated, which triggered this incredibly high fee. She and her moth=
er could face millions of dollars in debt. Her lawyer said that=E2=80=99s b=
ecause the label wanted to send a warning to other artists: If you try to l=
eave, this is what we can do to you.</span></p><table border=3D"0" cellpadd=
ing=3D"0" cellspacing=3D"0" style=3D"width:100%"><tbody><tr><td style=3D"te=
xt-align:center"><a href=3D"https://nl.nytimes.com/f/newsletter/G_3-K3kPM4N=
ikkaV17lh9Q~~/AAAAARA~/2ESakSHo5_DNiKsCVSABL7NLwdZwxjdR_zqku0p2WGx6nu77if7v=
