 Fei for The New York Times</span></span></td></tr></tbody></table><p style=
=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span s=
tyle=3D"font-weight:700">Sarah, how unique is this in the music industry=3F=
 We=E2=80=99ve heard stories about American pop stars who=E2=80=99ve dealt =
with exploitation from labels too, right=3F</span></p><p style=3D"font:norm=
al 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>What various p=
eople have told me is that the system today in South Korea is similar to a =
system that existed a long time ago in the U.S., in which, basically, one p=
erson or one company acts as label, manager, agent and lawyer, controlling =
virtually every facet of an artist=E2=80=99s career. That=E2=80=99s mostly =
no longer the case in the U.S. There are separate lawyers, agents and manag=
ers who represent the artist=E2=80=99s interests, not the label=E2=80=99s. =
In South Korea, the label still holds all the reins.</span></p><p style=3D"=
font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=
=3D"font-weight:700">Is there something that makes the K-pop industry parti=
cularly prone to this sort of hyper-controlling contracts, Yu Young=3F</spa=
n></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0=
 15px"><span>I think some of it is cultural. South Korea is a country that =
has developed very fast in the past half century. The concept of sacrifice =
for success is very ingrained. This idea that someone gives up everything f=
or a dream is not uncommon and it starts very young.</span></p><p style=3D"=
font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But s=
ome of it is related to the business model. Only a tiny percentage of aspir=
ing stars make it. The labels invest lots of money in dozens and dozens of =
young people who they train, most of whom will never earn that money back. =
So when one actually becomes a star, their success has to cover the costs o=
f all the others who didn=E2=80=99t.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px=
/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:=
700">So Sarah, this is basically a business model=E2=80=A6</span></p><p sty=
le=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span=
>The labels see these contracts as a way to make sure they get a return on =
their investment. They can spend hundreds of thousands of dollars building =
a group, and they make that money back mostly through tours and brand deals=
=2E So from their side, if an artist gains weight or gets into an =E2=80=9C=
inappropriate=E2=80=9D romantic relationship, that could hurt their image, =
and their profits.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,seri=
f;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">Is anyone try=
ing to reform the industry in response to all this=3F</span></p><p style=3D=
"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Ther=
e=E2=80=99s been some push for reform, but it ultimately hasn=E2=80=99t cha=
nged much. For example, one member of a really famous boy band tried to for=
